@meta
id: 30-02
title: Follow the import, then come back
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: goto-definition
par: 64
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-click the import path, then the back arrow
vimrc: set suffixesadd=.ts hidden
open: order.resolver.ts

@brief
The resolver calls `cancel` with one argument. The provider's `cancel`
has grown a second one — the cancellation reason — and nobody updated
the caller, so cancellations are landing with `undefined` as the reason.

Go and read the provider's signature, come back, and pass
`args.reason` as the second argument. The resolver is the only file
that changes; the provider is correct as it stands.

Vim knows how to open the file named under the cursor. It does not know
that `./order.provider` means `./order.provider.ts`, so `suffixesadd`
is set for this lesson — the same line you would keep in an
`after/ftplugin/typescript.vim`.

@keys
| `gf` | edit the file whose name is under the cursor |
| `Ctrl-W f` | the same, in a split |
| `Ctrl-O` | back to where you jumped from |
| `Ctrl-I` | forward again |
| `[{` | to the `{` that opens the block you are inside |

@hints
Put the cursor anywhere on the import path at the top of the file and
press `gf`.
--
`Ctrl-O` walks back down the jump list, and opening a file with `gf`
puts an entry on it. You do not have to remember what you were reading.
--
`/order\.provider` `Enter`, `gf`, read the signature, `Ctrl-O`, then
`/cancel(args` `Enter`, `f)`, `i, args.reason`, `Esc`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    /order\.provider<Enter> gf   Ctrl-O
    /cancel(args<Enter> f) i , args.reason <Esc> :wqa<Enter>

Three separate ideas are doing the work here.

`gf` resolves a file name the way a shell would: it takes the text
under the cursor, applies `suffixesadd` and `path`, and edits what it
finds. On an import line that is go-to-definition for the whole module.

`Ctrl-O` is the back button. Every jump — a search, `gf`, `G`, a tag —
pushes your old position onto the jump list, so the round trip out to a
definition and back is two keystrokes in each direction.

`[{` is the one people miss. Deep inside a long method, it puts you on
the brace that opened the block, which in practice means "show me the
signature of the thing I am editing". `]}` is its other half.

What `gf` cannot do is find the definition of a *name* rather than a
file. `OrderProvider` is imported by path, so the path is enough. A
helper function three directories away, imported nowhere near where you
are standing, is not. That is the job the next lesson gives to a tags
file.

@start order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string; reason: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string; reason: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id, args.reason);
  }
}

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string, reason: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED', cancellationReason: reason },
    });
  }
}
